{"metric":"brane_wall_universe","source":[{"metric":"brane_wall_universe","system":"static","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"f94f001f2add0c33"}],"views":[{"id":"kept","label":"One side","box":[-3.0317,3.0317,-1.8208,1.8208],"settings":"$\\ell = 1$, $\\mu = \\ell^2$, so that $r_h = \\ell$, and $k = 0$.","input":"A wall with no matter beside its tension, $\\rho = 0$, a flat space on it, $k = 0$, and a black hole of $\\mu = \\ell^2$, so that $r_h = \\ell$. It moves as $(da/d(c\\tau))^2 = \\mu/a^2$, so $a^2 = 2\\ell c\\tau$, and at the static time $ct = a + r_* - \\ell\\arctan(a/\\ell)$, with the tortoise radius $r_* = (\\ell/4)\\ln|(r - \\ell)/(r + \\ell)| + (\\ell/2)\\arctan(r/\\ell)$ at $r = a$.","caption":["One side of a wall that moves past the planar black hole of anti-de Sitter space in five dimensions, drawn for a wall with no matter beside its tension ($\\mu = \\ell^2$, $k = 0$), each point in the diagram a flat 3-space. The wall comes out of the past singularity at its middle, crosses the past horizon, and runs up to the corner where the conformal boundary meets the future singularity, which it reaches only as its universe grows without end. The side kept is the one where $r$ falls away from the wall, and on the far side of the wall lies its mirror image.","The side kept holds the black hole, the part of the white hole behind the wall, and the other exterior whole, out to its own conformal boundary. The wall stays below the future horizon of the black hole, so a light ray sent into the bulk from the wall falls through that horizon, and no signal from the black hole or the other exterior reaches the wall."],"layers":[{"kind":"fill","class":"region","points":[[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[-0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[-0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,-1.5